This line is the direct, one-to-one implementation of the reparameterized single-step diffusion formula from Section 1: xt​=1−βt​​xt−1​+βt​​ϵ. The current_tensor on the right-hand side represents xt−1​, and after the operation, it is updated to become the new state, xt​.The print statement inside the loop provides a live view of the process, showing how the tensor's mean and standard deviation evolve over time. Initially, these values will reflect the original tensor's properties. As the loop progresses and more noise is added, the mean will trend towards 0 and the standard deviation will trend towards 1.Output and ValidationAfter the loop completes, the current_tensor holds the final state, xT​. The script prints the final mean and standard deviation of this tensor. For a successful forward diffusion process, these final statistics should be very close to 0 and 1, respectively, which are the defining properties of a standard normal distribution. This serves as a quantitative validation that the initial data structure has been completely replaced by noise, as intended by the theory.Section 5: Advanced Analysis and Alternative ApproachesHaving implemented and dissected the iterative forward diffusion process, we can now place this knowledge in a broader context. This section compares the iterative method with the more efficient closed-form approach, discusses how to visualize the process, and, most importantly, explains how the forward process is fundamentally linked to the training objective of a diffusion model.Visualizing the Trajectory of NoiseFor tensors that represent images (e.g., with a shape of ``), visualizing the intermediate states provides a powerful and intuitive understanding of how information is destroyed. An extension to the script could involve saving the current_tensor at various timesteps (e.g., t=0,100,500,999).A potential implementation snippet using torchvision and PIL would look like this:Python# Inside the loop, after the update step
